Z||000009||BigBoyToys||02-07-2001||10:27 PM||||MM,<BR>It does sound electrical by the fact that you indicate that moisture brings on these symptoms. A bad connection that is corroded will create a much higher impedance (limiting current) when moist than when dry. The ecm oftem makes its calulations based on voltage or current drops/peaks across specific components. So any changes in these nominal readings will effect these calculations calculations. <P>Without getting to into it I would first suggest start it up in total darkness with the engine deck open looking to see if the ignition wires are sparking or any other wires/cables. Then under good light with engine off check connectors for pin corrosion (green crude). Funny thing about the GM weather tight connectors is that if they get wet inside (often from condensation when having set season after season) they rot out from the inside. Also since water and oil do not mix, and oil does not contribute to rust I often spray connectors and contacts with WD-40 then smear with a dielectric grease before reconnecting. You may have noticed that tailight sockets have a grease in them, it keeps the water out and thus limits the potential for corrosion. Check each fuse connection, often water gets behind the block. Check the fuselinks.<P>The signal the ECM receives is only as good as the conductors (including the connectors) will allow the signal to be, so even the signal from a brand new sensor (tps,map...) will not be received correctly if its path to the ECM is being impeded. <P>For example if you where to change your battery cables to speaker wire they would burn up trying to drive all the current the starter is requesting. <BR>Suppose a ECM signal wire is made up of 10 strands of copper wire and the insulation has been nicked at some point exposing the 10 strands of wire, and suppose 5 of the strands are also broke. Now that signal must pass by that point in the cable and still not loose its strength. Now add moisture and the green crud all over the 5 strands that are still connected even more signal lose. When the signal does finally get to the ECM it is not what the component had sent. The ECM makes it s calculation based on it's input and sends out the incorrect signal to another controller.  <P>Without the proper electrical test equipment and the knowledge of how to use them these problems can be very difficult to troubleshoot. As I have suggested inspect and correct those things that are most obvious. Slowly target small areas with WD or Wire-dri until you notice a positive change then target that area for poor connections.<P>Sorry for no quick fix! Best luck.    ||||reg||1
